Let $I=(c,d)$ with $-\infty\le c<0<d\le\infty$, and let $w:I\to(0,\infty)$ be an admissible exponential weight in the following sense. Write $Q=\log(1/w)$ and assume:
\begin{itemize}
\item $Q(0)=0$, $Q$ is continuous on $I$, $Q'$ exists and is nondecreasing on $I$, with $Q(x)\to\infty$ as $x\to c^+$ and as $x\to d^-$;
\item the function $T(x)=xQ'(x)/Q(x)$ is one-sided quasi-monotone on $I\setminus\{0\}$ and satisfies $\inf_{x\in I\setminus\{0\}} T(x)>1$;
\item there exists $\varepsilon_0\in(0,1)$ such that $T(y)$ is comparable to $T\!\left(y\bigl(1-\varepsilon_0/T(y)\bigr)\right)$ uniformly in $y$;
\item $Q'$ satisfies a local integral smoothness/Lipschitz-type bound of the standard admissibility form.
\end{itemize}
Let $f:I\to\mathbb{R}$ be a smooth function such that $fw$ decays at prescribed rates at the endpoints $c$ and $d$, and let $k:I\to\mathbb{R}$ be a kernel that decays at the endpoints in a prescribed manner. Consider:
(i) the ordinary integral $I[k,f]=\int_I f(t)k(t)\,dt$;
(ii) the weighted Hilbert-transform-type principal-value integral $I[k,f](x)=\mathrm{p.v.}\int_I \frac{f(t)w(t)}{x-t}\,dt$.
Given a triangular node array $\chi_n=\{x_{j,n}:1\le j\le n\}\subset I$, define the product-integration rule
$$I_n^*[k,f](x)=\sum_{j=1}^{n} w_{j,n}(x)\,f(x_{j,n}),$$
where the weights $w_{j,n}(x)$ are uniquely determined by the requirement that $I_n^*[k,P]=I[k,P]$ for every algebraic polynomial $P$ of degree at most $n-1$.

Find necessary and sufficient conditions on the admissible weight $w$, the kernel $k$, the function class for $f$ (specified by decay rates of $fw$ and smoothness), and the node arrays $\chi_n$ such that, as $n\to\infty$:
(a) $I_n^*[k,f]\to I[k,f]$ in suitable weighted $L^p$ (mean convergence) norms, and
(b) $I_n^*[k,f](x)\to I[k,f](x)$ pointwise on $I$,
for both the scalar and the weighted-Hilbert-transform versions of $I[k,f]$.

